Cap ykpiv chained command blocks at 254 data bytes.

Upstream _ykpiv_transfer_data fragments chained commands into 255-byte
blocks; on T=1 it appends an Le byte, so every full block travels as a
261-byte case-4S APDU (5 header + 255 data + 1 Le). The frozen jcardsim
3.0.6.0 emulator backs short APDUs with a 260-byte buffer (APDUProxy
BUFFER_SIZE) and throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException on the 261st
byte, which SimulatorRuntime.transmitCommand reports as SW 6F00. That
single ceiling breaks PIV certificate provisioning end to end: RSA
selfsign (chained GENERAL AUTHENTICATE) and every slot's
import-certificate (chained PUT DATA) all fail natively with 6F00,
while single-APDU operations succeed.

This patch caps chained blocks at 254 data bytes (5 + 254 + 1 = 260),
so no block can reach the emulator ceiling. ISO 7816 chaining permits
any block split, so the resulting traffic stays protocol-valid for any
peer, real or emulated; only transport fragmentation changes. No PIV
command, no authentication flow, no PIN/PUK handling, no certificate
content and no PKCS#11-visible behavior changes. The emulator keeps
its exact native behavior, including the 6F00 on 261-byte short
APDUs, which stays a documented unqualified surface.

Host-transport compatibility fix in frozen provisioning tooling: not
a provisioning extension (no new capability) and not a PIV, PKCS#11
or simulation semantic change. A declared patch that fails to apply
fails the build.

P11Lab-authored for the opensc-pivapplet provider recipe.
